COMPUTER SCIENCE AND INFORMATION THEORY THROUGH RAPIDITY
======================================================================

======================================================================
1. BINARY REPRESENTATIONS AND RAPIDITY
======================================================================

  A binary string of length n represents a number in [0, 2^n - 1].
  The information content = n bits = n * ln(2) nats.
  rapidity(2^n) = n * ln(2)/2 = n * octave.

  EACH BIT ADDS ONE OCTAVE OF RAPIDITY.
  This is the same as: each cell division adds one octave (biology).
  And: each DNA base adds one octave of sequence space.

  Standard data types and their rapidity:
  Type      Bits   Max value        rapidity(max)
  -------------------------------------------------------
  byte          8   2^8-1                  2.77 (8 octaves)
  short        16   2^16-1                  5.55 (16 octaves)
  int          32   2^32-1                 11.09 (32 octaves)
  long         64   2^64-1                 22.18 (64 octaves)
  uint128     128   2^128-1                 44.36 (128 octaves)
  uint256     256   2^256-1                 88.72 (256 octaves)

  Cryptographic key lengths in rapidity:
  AES-128: rapidity = 128 octaves
  AES-256: rapidity = 256 octaves
  RSA-2048: rapidity = 2048 octaves
  The security level IS the rapidity in octaves!

======================================================================
2. HASH FUNCTIONS AND BIRTHDAY PARADOX IN RAPIDITY
======================================================================

  A hash function with n-bit output has 2^n possible values.
  Birthday collision after ~2^{n/2} hashes.
  rapidity(collision threshold) = n/2 * ln(2)/2 = n*ln(2)/4
  = half the rapidity of the output space.

  For SHA-256: output space rapidity = 256 octaves.
  Collision threshold rapidity = 128 octaves.
  The birthday paradox HALVES the rapidity!

  For SHA-3-512: output space = 512 octaves, collision = 256.

======================================================================
3. SORTING AND COMPARISON COMPLEXITY
======================================================================

  Sorting n items requires at least log2(n!) comparisons.
  Stirling: log2(n!) ~ n*log2(n) - n*log2(e)
  In rapidity: rapidity(n!) = ln(n!)/2 ~ n*ln(n)/2 - n/2
  = n * rapidity(n) - n/2

  The information-theoretic lower bound for sorting
  IS the rapidity of n! (times 2/ln(2) for bits).

  n    n!           rapidity(n!)   log2(n!)    n*log2(n)
  ------------------------------------------------------------
    2               2         0.35           1.0           2.0
    3               6         0.90           2.6           4.8
    4              24         1.59           4.6           8.0
    5             120         2.39           6.9          11.6
    7            5040         4.26          12.3          19.7
   10         3628800         7.55          21.8          33.2
   20  2432902008176640000        21.17          61.1          86.4
   52          (huge)        76.73         221.4         296.4

  Sorting a deck of 52 cards: rapidity ~ 116.
  Compare: rapidity of the observable universe in Planck units ~ 140.
  A deck of cards is 83% of the universe's rapidity!

======================================================================
4. CHANNEL CAPACITY AND RAPIDITY
======================================================================

  Shannon's channel capacity: C = B * log2(1 + SNR)
  where B = bandwidth, SNR = signal-to-noise ratio.

  In rapidity: log2(1+SNR) = 2*rapidity(SNR/(SNR+2)) / ln(2)
  Actually: 1+SNR = Q(SNR/(SNR+2)), so
  log2(1+SNR) = 2*rapidity(SNR/(SNR+2)) / ln(2)

  But more directly: define v = (SNR)/(SNR+2) = (1+SNR-1)/(1+SNR+1).
  Then Q(v) = 1+SNR, and rapidity(v) = ln(1+SNR)/2.
  So C/B = 2*rapidity(v)/ln(2).

  Channel capacity per bandwidth = rapidity / (ln(2)/2).

  SNR(dB)  SNR(linear)  v=SNR/(SNR+2)  rapidity   C/B (bits/Hz)
  -----------------------------------------------------------------
      0            1.0      0.333333      0.3466        1.00
      3            2.0      0.499407      0.5485        1.58
      6            4.0      0.665612      0.8028        2.32
     10           10.0      0.833333      1.1989        3.46
     20          100.0      0.980392      2.3076        6.66
     30         1000.0      0.998004      3.4544        9.97
     40        10000.0      0.999800      4.6052       13.29
     60      1000000.0      0.999998      6.9078       19.93

======================================================================
5. ERROR CORRECTING CODES AND RAPIDITY
======================================================================

  A code with rate R = k/n transmits k bits over n symbols.
  The code rate R in [0,1] has rapidity arctanh(2R-1).

  Shannon limit: R < C/B for reliable transmission.
  In rapidity: rapidity(2R-1) < rapidity(2C/B - 1).

  For binary symmetric channel with crossover p:
  Capacity C = 1 - H(p) where H(p) = -p*log2(p) - (1-p)*log2(1-p)

  Common code rates and their rapidity:
  Rate    rapidity    interpretation
  ---------------------------------------------
    1/2    +0.0000     standard
    2/3    +0.3466     standard
    3/4    +0.5493     standard
    4/5    +0.6931     high rate
    7/8    +0.9730     high rate
    1/3    -0.3466     repetition
    1/4    -0.5493     repetition

  The rapidity of 1/2 rate = 0 (the FAIR POINT).
  Above 1/2: positive rapidity (more data than redundancy).
  Below 1/2: negative rapidity (more redundancy than data).

======================================================================
6. FLOATING POINT AND RAPIDITY
======================================================================

  IEEE 754 floating point: x = m * 2^e (mantissa * 2^exponent).
  The EXPONENT e IS a rapidity (in octaves):
  rapidity(x) = ln(x)/2 = (e*ln(2) + ln(m))/2
  ~ e * ln(2)/2 = e * octave (for m ~ 1)

  So floating point representation IS rapidity representation!
  The exponent = rapidity in octaves.
  The mantissa = fine structure within the octave.

  This is WHY floating point works well for physical quantities:
  physical laws involve RATIOS (rapidity differences),
  and floating point preserves rapidity structure.

  Float types and their rapidity range:
  Type       Exp bits   Rapidity range (octaves)
  --------------------------------------------------
  float16         5      [-14, 15]
  float32         8      [-126, 127]
  float64        11      [-1022, 1023]
  float128       15      [-16382, 16383]

======================================================================
7. PROBABILITY CALIBRATION AND RAPIDITY
======================================================================

  A classifier outputs probability p. Its calibration measures
  how well p matches the true frequency of positive outcomes.

  In rapidity space, perfect calibration means:
  rapidity(predicted) = rapidity(observed)

  The calibration ERROR in rapidity:
  delta_rapidity = arctanh(2*p_pred - 1) - arctanh(2*p_obs - 1)

  This is BETTER than raw probability difference because:
  - It handles extreme probabilities correctly (0.001 vs 0.002 is big!)
  - It's symmetric in log-odds space
  - It's equivalent to the natural parameter of the Bernoulli family

  Example: classifier on rare events
  Predicted  Observed  Raw diff   Rapidity diff
  --------------------------------------------------
  0.010      0.020     -0.0100     -0.3516
  0.100      0.110     -0.0100     -0.0532
  0.500      0.550     -0.0500     -0.1003
  0.900      0.910     -0.0100     -0.0582
  0.990      0.995     -0.0050     -0.3491

  Rapidity calibration reveals that 0.01->0.02 is a MUCH bigger
  error than 0.50->0.55, unlike raw probability difference.

======================================================================
8. SEARCH ALGORITHMS AND RAPIDITY
======================================================================

  Binary search on n items: log2(n) comparisons.
  = 2*rapidity(n)/ln(2) comparisons.

  Each binary search step HALVES the search space,
  which SUBTRACTS one octave of rapidity.
  Binary search = walking DOWN the rapidity ladder, one octave at a time.

  Grover's quantum search: sqrt(n) evaluations.
  = n^{1/2} evaluations.
  rapidity(sqrt(n)) = rapidity(n)/2.
  Grover's algorithm HALVES the rapidity (not the search space).

  Comparison:
  n         Binary: log2(n)   Grover: sqrt(n)   Classical: n
  ------------------------------------------------------------
  2^ 4             4                    4                  16
  2^ 8             8                   16                 256
  2^16            16                  256               65536
  2^20            20                 1024             1048576
  2^30            30                32768          1073741824

  Binary search: rapidity decreases by 1 octave per step.
  Grover: rapidity decreases by HALF per step (quadratic speedup).
  Linear search: rapidity decreases by 0 per step (just decrement).

======================================================================
9. BLOCKCHAIN AND PROOF OF WORK
======================================================================

  Bitcoin mining: find nonce such that SHA256(block||nonce) < target.
  The target has d leading zero bits.
  Expected attempts: 2^d.
  rapidity(2^d) = d * ln(2)/2 = d octaves.

  The mining DIFFICULTY in rapidity = d octaves.
  Each additional bit of difficulty = one more octave of work.

  Bitcoin difficulty adjustment: target rate = 1 block / 10 min.
  If blocks come too fast, increase d (add rapidity).
  If blocks come too slow, decrease d (remove rapidity).
  The difficulty IS a rapidity dial.

  Current Bitcoin difficulty ~ 2^76:
  rapidity = 76 octaves = 76 * ln(2)/2 = 26.3 rapidity units

======================================================================
10. THE RAPIDITY OF ALGORITHMIC COMPLEXITY CLASSES
======================================================================

  For input size n, the runtime of different complexity classes:

  Class        Runtime f(n)     rapidity(f(n))
  --------------------------------------------------
  O(1)         constant         0 (at rest)
  O(log n)     log n            ln(ln(n))/2 (doubly slow)
  O(n)         n                ln(n)/2 (linear in rapidity)
  O(n log n)   n*log(n)         ln(n*log(n))/2
  O(n^2)       n^2              ln(n) (quadratic = double rapidity)
  O(n^3)       n^3              3*ln(n)/2 (triple rapidity)
  O(2^n)       2^n              n*ln(2)/2 (linear in n octaves!)
  O(n!)        n!               n*ln(n)/2 - n/2 (superlinear)

  KEY INSIGHT: polynomial classes have rapidity ~ c*ln(n)/2 = c*rapidity(n).
  Exponential classes have rapidity ~ c*n = LINEAR in n (not log n).
  The P vs NP question in rapidity:
  P:  rapidity(runtime) = O(rapidity(n))
  NP: rapidity(runtime) = O(n) (rapidity grows with INPUT, not log)
  The gap between P and NP IS the gap between logarithmic and linear rapidity.

======================================================================
11. REED-SOLOMON CODES AND THE CAYLEY TRANSFORM
======================================================================

  Reed-Solomon codes evaluate polynomials at roots of unity.
  We proved: Q maps the n-th root of unity e^{2pi*i*k/n}
  to i*cot(pi*k/n) on the imaginary axis.

  So the Cayley transform of a Reed-Solomon codeword is a
  set of points on the imaginary axis!

  The SYNDROME computation (detecting errors) becomes:
  evaluation of Q-transformed polynomial at cotangent points.

  This might give a new perspective on RS decoding:
  errors in the codeword = perturbations of cotangent values.
  The error-locator polynomial in Cayley coordinates might
  have nicer properties than in the standard formulation.

======================================================================
12. MACHINE LEARNING: TEMPERATURE SCALING = RAPIDITY SCALING
======================================================================

  In softmax with temperature T:
  P_i = exp(z_i/T) / sum exp(z_j/T)

  For binary classification (2 classes):
  P = sigmoid(z/T) = sigmoid(logit/T)
  Since logit = 2*rapidity:
  P = sigmoid(2*rapidity/T)

  Temperature scaling DIVIDES the rapidity by T.
  T = 1: standard rapidity
  T > 1: compressed rapidity (less confident)
  T < 1: expanded rapidity (more confident)
  T -> 0: argmax (infinite rapidity expansion)
  T -> inf: uniform (rapidity compressed to 0)

  MODEL CALIBRATION by temperature scaling:
  Find T* that minimizes calibration error IN RAPIDITY SPACE.
  This is EXACTLY the standard temperature scaling recipe,
  but the rapidity interpretation shows WHY it works:
  you're finding the right rapidity SCALE for the problem.

======================================================================
GRAND SUMMARY: CS THROUGH RAPIDITY
======================================================================

  CS CONCEPT               RAPIDITY IS
  -------------------------------------------------------
  bits                     octaves of rapidity
  floating point exponent  rapidity in octaves
  binary search step       -1 octave
  Grover quantum step      -1/2 of remaining rapidity
  sorting lower bound      rapidity of n!
  channel capacity         rapidity / (ln(2)/2)
  code rate                arctanh(2R-1)
  hash collision           half the output rapidity
  mining difficulty        d octaves
  P vs NP gap              log(rapidity) vs linear rapidity
  temperature scaling      rapidity division by T
  calibration error        rapidity difference

  EVERYTHING in CS that uses log2 is using rapidity in octaves.
  EVERYTHING that uses log-odds is using rapidity directly.
  The bit IS the octave. The logit IS twice the rapidity.
